    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The flow of oil-in-water macroemulsions through porous media and capillary tubes was experimentally studied at room temperature and was described mathematically. The oil-in-water macroemulsions were prepared by using Chevron mineral oil No. 3 as the oil phase and brine solution (1% sodium chloride) as the aqueous phase. Triton N-57 was used as emulsifier at concentrations ranging from 0.50 to 1.00 cm per 100 cm^3 of oil. The test section of the flow system consisted of natural sandstone cores of different permeabilities encased in epoxy resin core holders; capillary tubes were directly attached to the core holder by using polyethylene fittings. Other connections were made in the core holder which allowed the measurement of pressure differentials across them. All the connections between the parts of the flow system consisted of polyethylene tubing. The flow system also includes a new electrical start-stop flow meter which simplifies and allows a faster determination of flow rates. The main part of the electrical flow meter is an array of relays which, when activated, can start and stop a timer as the fluid level rises in a vertical tube and passes through two pairs of silver electrodes. A new method of correlation which brings into superposition porous media and viscometer rheograms was developed. The method of correlation was based upon the experimental fact that the flow of macroemulsions through this kind of porous media and through capillary tubes exhibits the same rheological behavior. The correlation technique leads to a general equation which reduces to Darcy's law for Newtonian fluids. The average relative error found in applying the correlation was +/- 4%. The rheology of the macroemulsions of qualities (volumetric concentrations of the oil phase) ranging from 10 to 705 was obtained by using capillary tubes. Shear rates used in the experiments ranged from 1,320 to 10,300 sec. It was found that macroemulsions of quality lower than 503 behaved as Newtonian fluids, whereas those of qualities greater than 50% behaved as non-Newtonian pseudoplastic fluids. Viscoelastic effects were not observed for these macroemulsions. The particle size and size distribution of the macroemulsions were determined with a Coulter Counter Nodel TA. The particle diameter which ranged between 1 to 30 microns, was found to be log-normally distributed. Particle sizes and size distributions of emulsion samples taken before and after flow through porous media having average pore constriction size smaller than the emulsion droplets show little difference. The mechanism of the flow of macroemulsions under these conditions was explained by changes in droplet shape produced when an emulsion droplet transverses a pore constriction within the porous media.",
